  "final_text": "The dashboard and the alert rule both exist, and the rule is enabled. I haven't opened the dashboard or waited for the rule to run, so neither has been checked beyond the create calls succeeding. The rule's status is still \"pending\".\n\n**What the data holds:** the web-logs data view has one document per HTTP request. The main fields are:\n- `timestamp`, the time field.\n- `response`, the status code. It is stored as a string.\n- `bytes`, `extension`, `request`, `url` and `clientip`.\n- `geo.src`, `geo.dest` and `geo.coordinates`.\n- `machine.os`, `host`, `agent` and `referer`.\n\n**Dashboard:** \"EFF-4242abce Web logs overview\" has four panels:\n- **Total requests:** a single count of all requests.\n- **Requests over time:** a line chart of request counts by `timestamp`.\n- **Requests by response code:** a pie chart split by `response.keyword`.\n- **Average bytes by extension:** a bar chart of average `bytes` per `extension.keyword`.\n\nIt is at http://localhost:15601/app/dashboards#/view/eff-4242abce-web-logs-overview-7d8fab41ad78\n\n**Alert rule:** \"EFF-4242abce 5xx spike\" (id `12f51607-375c-4438-acf1-59042cdc3775`):\n- **Type:** an Elasticsearch query rule on `kibana_sample_data_logs`.\n- **Schedule:** it checks every minute.\n- **Condition:** it counts documents where `response.keyword` matches `5[0-9][0-9]`, over a 5-minute window. It fires when that count is above 10.\n\nI did not attach any actions, so the rule will show alerts in Kibana but won't send notifications anywhere. Also, the sample data's timestamps are shifted to be recent when it is loaded, so the rule may not fire if the sample data is old.",
